WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.230
Joan Celia Lee, who often goes by JC Lee,
the daughter of the late, great Marvel mastermind

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:09.920
Stan Lee, had some biting words for Marvel
Studios parent company Disney after it failed

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.060
to reach an agreement with Sony Pictures to
keep Spider-Man in the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:18.400
Lee spoke with TMZ about the situation, and
it's clear that she's not happy over what

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.140
she perceives to be a Disney power play to
score full control of Marvel Comics' flagship

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.689
character, whom Stan Lee co-created with Steve
Ditko, and for whom Sony has long held the

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:28.340
film rights.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.120
She said:

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.420
"Marvel and Disney seeking total control of
my father's creations must be checked and

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.170
balanced by others who, while still seeking
to profit, have genuine respect for Stan Lee

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:38.960
and his legacy.

00:00:39.449 --> 00:00:43.780
Whether it's Sony or someone else's, the continued
evolution of Stan's characters and his legacy

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.440
deserves multiple points of view."

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.140
Lee then went from implying Marvel Studios
did not have respect for Stan the Man's legacy

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.560
to outright stating it.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.220
"When my father died, no one from Marvel or
Disney reached out to me.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.859
From day one, they have commoditized my father's
work and never shown him or his legacy any

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:01.129
respect or decency…

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:05.400
In the end, no one could have treated my father
worse than Marvel and Disney's executives."

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.040
That's a brutal assessment, and, frankly,
confusing, considering that Stan Lee made

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.320
cameos in every single Marvel Studios film
up until his death.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.080
Publicly, Marvel head honcho Kevin Feige has
never shown anything but respect for Lee and

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:18.760
his legacy.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.060
After the icon's death last year, Feige said
in a statement,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.640
"He was very special, and I've always said,
anybody who met Stan…I've never heard one

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:30.609
story of somebody meeting Stan and not being
overwhelmed with excitement.

00:01:30.609 --> 00:01:34.579
He never disappointed, and I was lucky enough
to see him about 10 days before he passed

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:36.780
away… it doesn't seem like he's gone.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.100
Because just like with Walt Disney on this
lot, his influence will never go away."

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.939
JC Lee's comments came the same week as a
Sony Pictures statement which painted the

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.420
decision to axe the wall-crawler from the
MCU as one which ultimately was made by Marvel.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:50.240
It read:

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.119
"We are disappointed, but respect Disney's
decision not to have [Feige] continue as a

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.960
lead producer of our next live-action Spider-Man
film.

00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:00.999
We hope this might change in the future, but
understand that the many new responsibilities

00:02:00.999 --> 00:02:05.149
that Disney has given him - including all
their newly added Marvel properties - do not

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:07.940
allow time for him to work on IP they do not
own,"

00:02:08.549 --> 00:02:12.760
Inside sources, meanwhile, have insisted that
the split is solely over money, with Disney

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.480
having sought a bigger piece of the Spider-Pie
going forward.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.020
"Not get outta here."

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:20.520
"100 bucks?

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.160
The ad said 3,000."

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.340
This struggle over Spider-Man is likely far
from over.

00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:29.030
The reported split between the two studios
- which reached their initial agreement to

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:34.349
share custody of Spidey in 2015 - caused a
massive outcry among fans, most of whom were

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.400
far more pleased with the MCU iteration of
the character portrayed by Tom Holland than

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.680
they had been with the Amazing Spider-Man
franchise, which was considered a commercial

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:43.680
and critical failure.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.510
"Ho ho ho!

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.950
Well, look who's here!

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:47.700
Spidey!

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.280
How was your morning?

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.020
Aces, Max.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.000
Aces."

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.560
Of course, the failure of the two studios
to reach an agreement isn't final.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:01.760
The 2015 deal was struck after talks between
Sony and Marvel similarly fell apart in 2014,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.230
a situation that came to light when a series
of internal emails among Sony execs were made

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:10.250
public, and the strong reaction from fans
was undoubtedly a factor which brought the

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:11.460
parties back to the table.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:16.820
Sony maintains that it fully intends to produce
at least two more Spider-Man solo films starring

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:21.780
Holland, with Spider-Man: Homecoming and Spider-Man:
Far From Home director Jon Watts also returning.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:26.360
These films would presumably take place in
the Sony Universe of Marvel Characters, which

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.240
includes Venom.

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.160
That would mean the wholesale porting of a
character from one shared universe to another,

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.240
totally unrelated one.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:37.500
This has never happened before, in live action
anyway, and it would obviously present some

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.660
narrative challenges.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.380
To craft a satisfactory narrative around how
in the heck Spider-Man zapped to another universe,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.620
the two studios may have to cooperate, at
least very briefly.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.000
Perhaps prominent voices like Lee's can prod
the two companies to come back to the bargaining

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.240
table, because splitting may be more of a
headache than it's worth.

